Here's a complete Discord bot using **Discord.js v14** with a single slash command: `/ban`. This bot includes command registration, error handling, and permission checks.

---

### ✅ Requirements
- Node.js installed
- Discord.js v14
- A Discord bot with `Bot` and `Send Messages`, `Manage Messages`, and `Ban Members` permissions (in the server)

---

### 1. Initialize Your Project

```bash
npm init -y
npm install discord.js
```

---

### 2. Create `bot.js` (or `index.js`)

```javascript
const { Client, GatewayIntentBits, REST, Routes } = require('discord.js');
const dotenv = require('dotenv');
dotenv.config();

// Initialize client
const client = new Client({
  intents: [
    GatewayIntentBits.Guilds,
    GatewayIntentBits.GuildMembers,
    GatewayIntentBits.GuildMessages,
    GatewayIntentBits.MessageContent,
  ],
});

// Slash command configuration
const commands = [
  {
    name: 'ban',
    description: 'Ban a user from the server.',
    options: [
      {
        type: 6,
        name: 'user',
        description: 'The user to ban',
        required: true,
      },
      {
        type: 3,
        name: 'reason',
        description: 'Reason for the ban (optional)',
      },
    ],
  },
];

// Register slash commands (run once or on startup)
const rest = new REST({ version: '10' }).setToken(process.env.TOKEN);

const registerCommands = async () => {
  try {
    console.log('Registering slash commands...');
    await rest.put(Routes.applicationCommands(process.env.CLIENT_ID), { body: commands });
    console.log('Slash commands registered successfully!');
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error registering commands:', error);
  }
};

// Event: Ready
client.once('ready', () => {
  console.log(`Logged in as ${client.user.tag}!`);
  registerCommands();
});

// Event: Interaction Create (handle commands)
client.on('interactionCreate', async (interaction) => {
  if (!interaction.isChatInputCommand()) return;

  if (interaction.commandName === 'ban') {
    const user = interaction.options.getUser('user');
    const reason = interaction.options.getString('reason') || 'No reason provided';

    // Check if the bot has the required permission
    if (!interaction.guild.members.me.permissions.has('BanMembers')) {
      return interaction.reply({ content: 'I do not have permission to ban members.', ephemeral: true });
    }

    // Check if the bot can ban the user (user must be lower role than bot)
    if (interaction.guild.members.me.roles.highest.position <= interaction.guild.members.cache.get(user.id)?.roles.highest.position) {
      return interaction.reply({ content: 'I cannot ban this user because their role is higher or equal to mine.', ephemeral: true });
    }

    // Check if the user has permission to ban (only admins/mods)
    if (!interaction.member.permissions.has('BanMembers')) {
      return interaction.reply({ content: 'You do not have permission to use this command.', ephemeral: true });
    }

    try {
      await interaction.guild.members.ban(user, { reason: reason });
      await interaction.reply({
        content: `✅ Successfully banned ${user.tag} for: ${reason}`,
        ephemeral: false,
      });
      console.log(`User ${user.tag} was banned by ${interaction.user.tag}`);
    } catch (error) {
      console.error('Error banning user:', error);
      await interaction.reply({
        content: 'Failed to ban the user. Please check permissions and try again.',
        ephemeral: true,
      });
    }
  }
});

// Login to Discord
client.login(process.env.TOKEN);
```

---

### 3. `.env` File (Create this in your project root)

```env
CLIENT_ID=your-bot-client-id
TOKEN=your-bot-token
```

> Replace `your-bot-client-id` and `your-bot-token` with the actual values from the [Discord Developer Portal](https://discord.com/developers/applications).

---

### 4. Run Your Bot

```bash
node bot.js
```

---

### 🔐 Permissions Required

Make sure your bot has the following permissions in the server:
- **Ban Members** (to ban users)
- **Send Messages** (to respond)

And the user who uses `/ban` must have **Ban Members** permission.

---

### ✅ Example Usage

```bash
/ban user:@username reason:Breaking rules
```

---

### 💡 Optional Improvements

- Add moderator role checks
- Handle guild-specific commands
- Use a command handler for multiple commands later
- Add logging to a file or external service

