Here's a C++ function that converts Markdown-formatted text to HTML. This implementation handles basic Markdown features like headings, bold, italic, links, and lists, but note that it's a simplified version and doesn't cover all Markdown syntax (like tables, code blocks, etc.).

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <regex>
#include <iostream>

std::string markdownToHtml(std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html = markdown;

    // Fix potential double-newlines that break formatting
    // Replace all \n\n with <p>...</p> or <br> in list items
    // But first, we preprocess to handle paragraph breaks

    // 1. Handle code blocks (backticks)
    std::regex codeRegex("`([^`]+)`");  // single backticks
    html = std::regex_replace(html, codeRegex, "<code>$1</code>");

    std::regex codeBlockRegex("```([^`]*)```");  // triple backticks
    html = std::regex_replace(html, codeBlockRegex, "<pre><code>$1</code></pre>");

    // 2. Handle bold text (**bold** or __bold__)
    std::regex boldRegex("\\*\\*([^*]+)\\*\\*"); // **text**
    html = std::regex_replace(html, boldRegex, "<strong>$1</strong>");

    std::regex bold2Regex("__([^_]+)__/");  // __text__
    html = std::regex_replace(html, bold2Regex, "<strong>$1</strong>");

    // 3. Handle italic text (*italic* or _italic_)
    std::regex italicRegex("\\*([^*]+)\\*"); // *text*
    html = std::regex_replace(html, italicRegex, "<em>$1</em>");

    std::regex italic2Regex("_([^_]+)_");  // _text_
    html = std::regex_replace(html, italic2Regex, "<em>$1</em>");

    // 4. Handle headings (#, ##, ###, etc.)
    std::regex headingRegex("^#{1,6}\\s+(.+)$", std::regex::multiline);
    html = std::regex_replace(html, headingRegex, "<h$&>$1</h$&>");

    // 5. Handle links
    std::regex linkRegex("\\[([^\\]]+)\\]\\(([^)]+)\\)");
    html = std::regex_replace(html, linkRegex, "<a href=\"$2\">$1</a>");

    // 6. Handle list items (unordered and ordered)
    std::regex unorderedListRegex("^\\s*\\-\\s+(.+)$", std::regex::multiline);
    html = std::regex_replace(html, unorderedListRegex, "<li>$1</li>");

    std::regex orderedListRegex("^\\s*\\d+\\s+(.+)$", std::regex::multiline);
    html = std::regex_replace(html, orderedListRegex, "<li>$1</li>");

    // 7. Wrap list items in appropriate list tags
    // This is a simple approach; for fully correct parsing, consider a more sophisticated parser
    // For now, we assume lists are already correctly formatted

    // 8. Convert paragraphs: wrap lines with text and no markup (except for list items) in <p> tags
    // This requires more sophisticated logic, but here's a basic approach:
    std::regex paraRegex("([^\\n]+\\n?)");
    html = std::regex_replace(html, paraRegex, "<p>$1</p>");

    // 9. Fix line breaks for single line items
    std::regex linebreakRegex("\n");
    html = std::regex_replace(html, linebreakRegex, "<br>");

    return html;
}
```

### Important Notes:

- This implementation uses **`std::regex`** and is not complete — it only handles **basic Markdown syntax**.
- Special characters, edge cases, and nesting are not handled correctly.
- For example, the `#` in a heading should **only apply if it's at the start of a line** and followed by space — our regex `^#{1,6}\\s+(.+)$` is just a placeholder.
- Similarly, list items should **only apply if they start with `-` or a number**, and we don't track list context (e.g., nested lists).
- This regex-based approach is **not suitable for complex Markdown**. It breaks easily on edge cases.

---

### Recommended Alternative: Use a Markdown Library

For production use, use a library like [PegTL](https://github.com/taocpp/PEGTL) or a mature C++ Markdown parser such as [CMark](https://github.com/commonmark/cmark) (libcommonmark) or [MarkDown](https://github.com/toastdriven/pymdown-extensions).

But if you need a quick-and-dirty function, here's a **simpler version**:

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <regex>

std::string markdownToHtml(std::string& markdown) {
    std::string result = markdown;

    // Replace code blocks
    std::regex codeBlock("```([^`]+)```", std::regex_constants::icase);
    result = std::regex_replace(result, codeBlock, "<pre><code>$1</code></pre>");

    // Replace inline code
    std::regex code("(`)([^`]+)`", std::regex_constants::icase);
    result = std::regex_replace(result, code, "<code>$2</code>");

    // Bold
    std::regex bold("(\\*\\*)([^*]+)(\\*\\*))");
    result = std::regex_replace(result, bold, "<strong>$2</strong>");

    // Italic
    std::regex italic("(\\*)([^*]+)(\\*))");
    result = std::regex_replace(result, italic, "<em>$2</em>");

    // Heading (only if at start of line)
    std::regex heading("^#{1,6} (.*)", std::regex_constants::multiline);
    result = std::regex_replace(result, heading, "<h$&>$1</h$&>");

    // Link
    std::regex link("\\[(.*?)\\]\\((.*?)\\)");
    result = std::regex_replace(result, link, "<a href=\"$2\">$1</a>");

    return result;
}
```

> ⚠️ **Warning**: This function is **not robust**. For real use, consider using a well-tested Markdown parser.

