Here is an example of how to generate a table of contents automatically in a LaTeX document using standard LaTeX commands:

```latex
\documentclass[12pt]{article}

\title{Sample Document with Table of Contents}
\author{John Doe}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\tableofcontents

\section{Introduction}
This is the introduction section.

\section{Background}
This section provides background information.

\subsection{Subsection Example}
Details about the sub-section.

\section{Methodology}
This section describes the methodology.

\subsection{Data Collection}
Discussion on how data was collected.

\section{Results}
Presentation of results.

\section{Conclusion}
Summary of findings.

\end{document}
```

### Explanation:

- `\tableofcontents` — This command generates the table of contents automatically.
- `\section{}`, `\subsection{}` — These commands create sections and subsections. LaTeX will automatically number them and include them in the table of contents.
- `\maketitle` — Adds the title, author, and date to the document.
- The table of contents is generated **automatically** based on the sections and subsections defined in the document.

### Note:
- You must compile the document twice for the table of contents to be accurate. This is because LaTeX first collects the section titles and their page numbers during the first compilation pass, and then uses that information to generate the table of contents in the second pass.

This approach works in all standard LaTeX document classes like `article`, `report`, and `book`.

